  "worked_solution": "We say that an implication has a *vacuous proof* if the antecedent is always false: \n\n$$\n\\overset{\\text{Always false}}{\\overset{\u23de}{P}}\u21d2\\,Q\n$$\n\nIt does not matter whether the consequent is true or false. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our antecedents in turn.\n\n- Predicate I, defined for $x\\in Q,$ is always true. $\\,\\times $\n\n- Predicate II, defined for $x\\in Q,$ is *not* always false. $\\,\\times $\n\n- Predicate III, defined for $x\\in Q,$ is always false. $\\,\u2713$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
